<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · 1941 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Sacred War</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ru">Священная война</bdi> / <bdi lang="ru-Latn">Svyashchennaya voyna</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Soviet song written days after Germany invaded the Soviet Union in June 1941, calling people to defend their country.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Vasily Lebedev-Kumach (authorship disputed)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Alexander Alexandrov</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Soviet Union in the Second World War</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Moscow, Soviet Union</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>24–26 June 1941</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Vasily Lebedev-Kumach (1941); music by Alexander Alexandrov (d. 1946))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;The Sacred War&quot; is one of the best-known Soviet songs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> as the Great Patriotic War. Two days after the German invasion, on 24 June 1941, its words appeared at the same time in the newspapers Izvestia and Krasnaya Zvezda, signed by the poet Vasily Lebedev-Kumach. Alexander Alexandrov, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>'s song and dance ensemble and later the composer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-anthem-of-the-soviet-union">State Anthem of the Soviet Union</a>, set them to music at once. There was no time to print words and notes, so he wrote them in chalk on a blackboard and the singers and players copied them into their notebooks; one more day went to rehearsal.</p><p>On 26 June 1941 a group of the ensemble not yet sent to the front sang it for the first time at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>'s Belorussky Railway Station; eyewitnesses recalled that it was sung five times in a row. A memorial plaque was placed on the station building in May 2005. Yet until 15 October 1941 the song was not widely performed, as its tone was thought too tragic beside the hope of a quick victory at little cost. Only from that day, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> had already taken Kaluga, Rzhev and Kalinin, was it played every morning on all-Union radio after the chimes of the Kremlin clock. It became hugely popular at the front, especially in the hard defensive battles, and was recorded twice during the war, in 1941 and 1942.</p><p>After the war the Alexandrov Ensemble sang it often, at home and on tours abroad. On 22 May 2007 the ensemble performed it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> to loud applause; some Russian media read this as an ambiguous answer to Western attitudes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The song was translated into Ukrainian by Mykola Bazhan, into German as &quot;Der Heilige Krieg&quot; by Stephan Hermlin, and into Polish, best known in Krzysztof Gruszczyński's version; a Polish version that leaves out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and a Hungarian version are also known.</p><p>In the 1990s, after Soviet censorship ended, Russian media claimed that the words were really written during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> by Aleksandr Bode (1865–1939), an amateur poet and teacher of literature from Rybinsk, and that Lebedev-Kumach had plagiarised them. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> court ruled on 21 December 1999 that the claim was untrue and defamatory and that Lebedev-Kumach was the author, and in June 2000 the newspaper Nezavisimaya Gazeta was made to print a retraction. A draft in Lebedev-Kumach's hand, with many corrections, is reported to be in the Russian State Archive of Literature and Art. The musicologist Evgeniy Levashev nonetheless defended Bode's authorship, questioned the court's decision (it had refused the defendants an expert examination), and argued that the first melody was also Bode's, with Alexandrov as arranger. Against this, Alexandrov's own &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Kliment Voroshilov&quot; (words by Osip Kolychev, 1938) has a melody in which the musicologist A. N. Sokhor heard a forerunner of &quot;The Sacred War&quot;.</p><p>In 2018–2019 the Ukrainian kobzar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a> Sylenko found among the songs of insurgents who fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> for the Ukrainian People's Republic around 1920 a song whose melody is almost identical to Alexandrov's. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s full-scale invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> in 2022 his account gained new attention and was supported by the writer Yuri Andrukhovych. The music critic Lyubov Morozova suggested that Bode's authorship could explain it, the song reaching the people through his wife, a singer, and noted that its 3/4 time is most unusual for a Soviet march.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A call to the whole country to rise and fight the German invasion as a people's war; grim, stern and solemn.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius" data-genius="2059608"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press the button. <a href="https://genius.com/The-red-army-choir-the-sacred-war-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="F8FYEqhD4M4" data-title="The Sacred War: the Alexandrov Ensemble, whose founder wrote the music"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">the Alexandrov Ensemble, whose founder wrote the music</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel The Red Army Choir - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F8FYEqhD4M4" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Vstavay, krayino gordaya (Ukrainian translation by Mykola Bazhan)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Der Heilige Krieg (German translation by Stephan Hermlin)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Polish translation by Krzysztof Gruszczyński</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Żołnierzu do szeregu stań (Polish version)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as, without mention of the USSR</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fel, küzdelemre, hős Haza (Hungarian version)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Song of Kliment Voroshilov (Alexander Alexandrov, words by Osip Kolychev)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1938</span><span class="sg-chain-h">its melody heard as a forerunner (A. N. Sokhor)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Povstan, narode miy! (Os' den' viyny narodnoyi), Ukrainian insurgents' song</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1920 (claimed)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">almost identical melody, according to Taras Sylenko (disputed)</span></li></ul></section>
